// ── THE MEMORY ENVELOPE IS RESOLVED AGAINST OBSERVED SUPPLY, NOT DECIDED AGAINST CAPACITY ──────
//
// THE PRECEDING MODEL ASKED THE WRONG INSTRUMENT AND THE ANSWER WAS PLAUSIBLE. A single row named a
// fraction -- 0.90 -- and the launch multiplied it by the device's TOTAL memory to reason about fit. The
// engine does not admit against total; it reads FREE device memory at startup and refuses if its budget
// exceeds it. Those two readings differ by whatever else the machine is holding, and on this hardware the
// difference is large and moves: on 2026-09-10 two of four ranks reported 106.82 GiB free of 121.69 GiB
// total and refused a 109.52 GiB budget, while the host's own `free` reported about 117 GB available.
// Three instruments, three numbers, and the launch had consulted the only one with no authority over the
// decision. The class is rostered as
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.host_available_memory_read_as_device_admission.
//
// SO THE FRACTION IS NOT A VALUE ANY MODULE CARRIES. It is the RESULT of a fold over one fresh reading per
// planned rank, and the policy carries only what a policy can carry: a ceiling the operator will not
// exceed, a floor below which the arm is not worth launching, and a reserve between the budget and the
// supply. Everything between those is arithmetic over observations.
//
// THE CORRECTION WAS NEVER "USE A SMALLER NUMBER", AND THE FROZEN PROFILE IS NOT THAT EITHER. A tuned
// constant substituted for this model would be the same defect with a smaller value: green on the machine
// that produced it, wrong on the next one or on the same one an hour later.
//
// SO THIS MODULE ANSWERS TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND KEEPS THEM APART. RESOLUTION asks what the most a
// population can carry is, and is the authority for that; ADMISSION asks whether every rank can carry a
// fraction someone has already decided. The initial normal deployment uses the second, at an operator's
// 0.84 -- and the observation, the completeness join and every refusal are identical in both. What a
// decided fraction removes is the derivation, not the evidence: no rank is assumed, and a rank that cannot
// admit 0.84 refuses rather than quietly launching at whatever it could have taken.
type DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy sole_constructor {
  maximum_fraction_basis_points: Nat
  minimum_fraction_basis_points: Nat
  startup_reserve_bytes: ByteSize
  granularity_basis_points: Nat
}

// THE CEILING IS THE OPERATOR'S AND IT IS BELOW THE IMAGE'S DEFAULT. Read from the pinned digest on
// 2026-09-10, `gpu_memory_utilization: float = Field(default=0.92, gt=0, le=1)`; 0.90 is a deliberate and
// slightly more conservative maximum (operator, 2026-09-10). It is a CEILING here rather than a request,
// which is the only change that matters: nothing may resolve above it, and resolving below it is ordinary.
//
// THE FLOOR IS GROUNDED IN THE ONLY MEASURED REQUIREMENT THIS ARM HAS. 76.45 GiB of weights per rank
// against 121.69 GiB of device memory is 0.628 of the pool, and a full-window KV requirement of roughly
// 1.4 GiB for the whole group adds almost nothing. What is NOT measured is the activation, graph-capture
// and workspace peak, so the floor is the measured weight fraction plus a declared margin rather than a
// derived minimum -- 0.80 leaves about 21 GiB per rank above the weights. A launch resolved below that
// would be admitting a budget that has never been shown to hold a forward pass, and refusing is the
// honest arm: DERIVATION TRIGGER for replacing this margin with a measurement is the first startup memory
// profile captured from a rank that actually serves.
//
// THE RESERVE COVERS THE DRIFT BETWEEN THIS OBSERVATION AND THE ENGINE'S OWN ADMISSION, and it is now
// MEASURED rather than guessed. That is the whole of its job: the supply is read here, the engine reads it
// again moments later, and the launch itself changes the number in between.
//
// 4 GiB WAS BELOW THE DRIFT IT EXISTS TO ABSORB, and attempt 5 on 2026-09-11 is the receipt. Per host:
// releasing page cache gave 117 GiB free; this module's probe container reported 113.5 GiB; a 4 GiB reserve
// resolved (113.5 - 4) / 121.69 = 0.9002, which caps to the ceiling and renders 0.90 for a budget of 109.52
// GiB; and the engine then refused at 106.7 GiB free. The arithmetic was exact and the answer was 2.8 GiB too
// high, because between the two readings the container's own startup -- vLLM's modules, the model config, the
// safetensors index -- refilled page cache and took the device figure back down.
//
// THE DRIFT MEASURED DIRECTLY IS 6.8 GiB (113.5 observed here, 106.7 at the engine's check), taken on an idle
// host with caches released. 8 GiB carries that with 1.2 GiB to spare, which is the reserve's whole job.
//
// A FIRST REPAIR SET IT TO 12 GiB AND THAT WOULD HAVE REFUSED THE FROZEN PROFILE. 12 was the measured drift
// plus margin chosen when this reserve fed a RESOLUTION -- where a larger number only means a more
// conservative fraction. Under a DECIDED fraction the reserve stops being a tuning knob and becomes an
// admission threshold: at 12 GiB a released host resolves a cap of 8300 basis points, which is below the
// decided 8400, so every rank would have refused a launch that fits. Arithmetic caught it before a
// displacement window did. The reserve must cover the drift and nothing more, because whatever it covers
// beyond that is subtracted from a budget an operator has already decided.
//
// IT IS NOT SLACK FOR THE MODEL, and the two must not be confused. The CUDA context, the NCCL communicator
// buffers and the loader's transient allocations also live outside the fraction and draw on the same pool;
// what makes this figure large is the observation-to-admission drift, not those.
//
// NEXT DERIVATION: the drift is re-measurable on demand -- release, probe, start a container, read the
// engine's reported figure -- so this row's trigger is an observed drift per attempt rather than a standing
// number, at which point the reserve becomes another produced reading and this constant deletes.
//
// THE GRANULARITY IS A PROPERTY OF THE WIRE. `--gpu-memory-utilization` is rendered as a two-digit decimal
// fraction, so a resolution of 8449 basis points cannot be expressed and must floor to 8400. Flooring is
// the safe direction and saying so here keeps the renderer from silently truncating a value the fold
// believed it had chosen.
data glm_native_device_memory_policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy = DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy {
  maximum_fraction_basis_points: 9000,
  minimum_fraction_basis_points: 8000,
  startup_reserve_bytes: byte_size(count: 8589934592),
  granularity_basis_points: 100,
}

// ── ONE READING PER RANK, TAKEN THROUGH THE ENGINE'S OWN INSTRUMENT ────────────────────────────
//
// THE OBSERVATION CARRIES THE IMAGE AND THE ATTEMPT BECAUSE BOTH CAN MAKE IT WORTHLESS. A reading taken in
// a different image is a reading of a different allocator's view of the device, and a reading taken before
// the previous attempt's containers were removed describes memory that is no longer held. Neither
// mismatch is visible in the number itself, so the number is not allowed to travel without them.
type RankDeviceMemoryObservation sole_constructor {
  host: HostIdentity
  node_rank: Nat
  runtime_image: NonEmptyStr
  attempt: NonEmptyStr
  free_bytes: ByteSize
  total_bytes: ByteSize
}

// THE READING'S CONSTRUCTOR IS EXPORTED FOR THE SAME REASON THE RANK'S IS. The resolution's whole value is
// which populations it REFUSES, and a witness that cannot author a population -- a stale attempt, a foreign
// digest, a rank short of the floor -- cannot establish any of those refusals. So the observation is
// constructible from outside; what is NOT constructible from outside is a RankEnvelopeStanding, which remains
// derived from a reading by this module alone.
fn rank_device_memory_reading(
  host: HostIdentity, node_rank: Nat, runtime_image: NonEmptyStr, attempt: NonEmptyStr,
  free_bytes: ByteSize, total_bytes: ByteSize,
) -> RankDeviceMemoryReading {
  DeviceMemoryRead {
    observation: RankDeviceMemoryObservation {
      host: host, node_rank: node_rank, runtime_image: runtime_image,
      attempt: attempt, free_bytes: free_bytes, total_bytes: total_bytes,
    },
  }
}

type RankDeviceMemoryReading
  = DeviceMemoryRead { observation: RankDeviceMemoryObservation }
  | DeviceMemoryUnread { host: HostIdentity, node_rank: Nat, cause: String }

// THE PROBE RUNS INSIDE THE EXACT LAUNCH IMAGE AND ASKS `torch.cuda.mem_get_info`, WHICH IS THE FUNCTION
// THE ENGINE ITSELF CALLS. Every cheaper source was available and every one of them answers a different
// question: `free` and /proc/meminfo report host reclaimability, `nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.free`
// answers [N/A] on the GB10, and a value copied from an earlier run answers about an earlier moment. The
// cost of the honest instrument is one container start per rank, which is the cheapest part of a launch
// that loads 76 GiB of weights four times.
//
// THE ENTRYPOINT IS OVERRIDDEN BECAUSE THE IMAGE'S IS `vllm serve`. Running the image unmodified would
// start an engine, which is the thing this check exists to decide about.
fn device_memory_probe_argv(image: NonEmptyStr) -> List<String> {
  [
    "docker", "run", "--rm", "--gpus", "all", "--ipc", "host",
    "--entrypoint", "python3",
    image as String,
    "-c", "import torch;f,t=torch.cuda.mem_get_info();print(f);print(t)",
  ]
}

// TWO LINES, EACH A BARE RUN OF DIGITS, AND ANYTHING ELSE IS UNREAD. The probe prints free and total on
// separate lines so the reading needs no delimiter convention, and std.nat parse_nat refuses on anything
// that is not exactly digits -- so a warning line, an empty output, or a traceback becomes
// DeviceMemoryUnread rather than a number. A partial-prefix parse here would turn "0 MiB free, see
// warning" into a zero, and a zero free reading and an unreadable one demand opposite actions.
type DeviceMemoryPair sole_constructor {
  free_bytes: Nat
  total_bytes: Nat
}

fn read_probe_pair(lines: List<String>) -> DeviceMemoryPair? {
  match lines.first() {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: free_line } =>
      match lines.skip(n: 1).first() {
        Absent => none
        Present { value: total_line } =>
          match parse_nat(s: trim(s: free_line)) {
            Absent => none
            Present { value: f } =>
              match parse_nat(s: trim(s: total_line)) {
                Absent => none
                Present { value: t } => Present { value: DeviceMemoryPair { free_bytes: f, total_bytes: t } }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn classify_device_memory_reading(
  node: ArmNode, image: NonEmptyStr, attempt: NonEmptyStr, execution: SparkRemoteRun,
) -> RankDeviceMemoryReading {
  match execution {
    RemoteUnreachable { host: h, cause: cz } =>
      DeviceMemoryUnread { host: h, node_rank: node.node_rank, cause: cz }
    RemoteRan { host: h, exit_code: _, stdout: _, stderr: _ } =>
      if !remote_run_succeeded(r: execution) {
        DeviceMemoryUnread { host: h, node_rank: node.node_rank, cause: remote_run_text(r: execution) }
      } else {
        match read_probe_pair(lines: split(s: trim(s: remote_run_stdout(r: execution)), delimiter: "\n")) {
          Absent => DeviceMemoryUnread {
            host: h, node_rank: node.node_rank,
            cause: join(["probe output is not two digit runs: ", remote_run_text(r: execution)], ""),
          }
          Present { value: pair } => DeviceMemoryRead {
            observation: RankDeviceMemoryObservation {
              host: node.host,
              node_rank: node.node_rank,
              runtime_image: image,
              attempt: attempt,
              free_bytes: byte_size(count: pair.free_bytes),
              total_bytes: byte_size(count: pair.total_bytes),
            },
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

func observe_rank_device_memory(
  ctx: SparkRemoteExecutionContext, node: ArmNode, image: NonEmptyStr, attempt: NonEmptyStr,
) -> RankDeviceMemoryReading
  uses net: Network
{
  classify_device_memory_reading(
    node: node, image: image, attempt: attempt,
    execution: spark_exec(ctx: ctx, host: node.host, argv: device_memory_probe_argv(image: image)),
  )
}

func observe_device_memory_population(
  ctx: SparkRemoteExecutionContext, nodes: List<ArmNode>, image: NonEmptyStr, attempt: NonEmptyStr,
) -> List<RankDeviceMemoryReading>
  uses net: Network
{
  map(nodes, n => observe_rank_device_memory(ctx: ctx, node: n, image: image, attempt: attempt))
}

// ── EVERY RANK'S STANDING IS PRODUCED, NOT ASSERTED ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THIS IS THE HALF A CALLER-AUTHORED PRECONDITION ENUM CANNOT DO. An earlier cutover roster let the caller
// hand in a list of satisfied precondition tokens, which means the strongest statement the transaction
// could make was that somebody had typed the token. A standing that is DERIVED from an observation cannot
// be typed: it exists only if the reading exists, names the rank it came from, and carries the digest and
// attempt it was taken under.
type RankEnvelopeStanding
  = RankEnvelopeSupplies { node_rank: Nat, host: HostIdentity, cap_basis_points: Nat, free_bytes: ByteSize }
  | RankEnvelopeBelowFloor { node_rank: Nat, host: HostIdentity, cap_basis_points: Nat, floor_basis_points: Nat }
  | RankEnvelopeUnread { node_rank: Nat, host: HostIdentity, cause: String }
  | RankEnvelopeForeignImage { node_rank: Nat, host: HostIdentity, observed_image: NonEmptyStr, expected_image: NonEmptyStr }
  | RankEnvelopeStaleAttempt { node_rank: Nat, host: HostIdentity, observed_attempt: NonEmptyStr, expected_attempt: NonEmptyStr }

// THE CAP IS FLOORED TWICE AND BOTH FLOORS ARE DELIBERATE. Once to basis points, because the fraction of a
// supply is not generally a terminating decimal, and once to the wire's granularity, because a value that
// cannot be rendered cannot be launched. Both floor DOWN: rounding up would resolve a budget the rank has
// already been shown not to have.
//
// A RESERVE LARGER THAN THE FREE READING PRODUCES ZERO, NOT AN UNDERFLOW. A rank with less free memory
// than the reserve supplies nothing, and saying that with a zero cap lets it fail the floor check like any
// other insufficient rank rather than through a separate arm.
fn rank_cap_basis_points(o: RankDeviceMemoryObservation, policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy) -> Nat {
  let free = byte_size_count(b: o.free_bytes)
  let total = byte_size_count(b: o.total_bytes)
  let reserve = byte_size_count(b: policy.startup_reserve_bytes)
  if total == 0 || free <= reserve {
    (total - total)
  } else {
    let raw = ((free - reserve) * 10000) / total
    (raw / policy.granularity_basis_points) * policy.granularity_basis_points
  }
}

fn rank_envelope_standing(
  reading: RankDeviceMemoryReading, policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy,
  expected_image: NonEmptyStr, expected_attempt: NonEmptyStr,
) -> RankEnvelopeStanding {
  match reading {
    DeviceMemoryUnread { host: h, node_rank: r, cause: cz } =>
      RankEnvelopeUnread { node_rank: r, host: h, cause: cz }
    DeviceMemoryRead { observation: o } =>
      if (o.runtime_image as String) != (expected_image as String) {
        RankEnvelopeForeignImage {
          node_rank: o.node_rank, host: o.host,
          observed_image: o.runtime_image, expected_image: expected_image,
        }
      } else if (o.attempt as String) != (expected_attempt as String) {
        RankEnvelopeStaleAttempt {
          node_rank: o.node_rank, host: o.host,
          observed_attempt: o.attempt, expected_attempt: expected_attempt,
        }
      } else {
        let cap = rank_cap_basis_points(o: o, policy: policy)
        if cap < policy.minimum_fraction_basis_points {
          RankEnvelopeBelowFloor {
            node_rank: o.node_rank, host: o.host,
            cap_basis_points: cap, floor_basis_points: policy.minimum_fraction_basis_points,
          }
        } else {
          RankEnvelopeSupplies {
            node_rank: o.node_rank, host: o.host,
            cap_basis_points: cap, free_bytes: o.free_bytes,
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn rank_envelope_standing_text(s: RankEnvelopeStanding) -> String {
  match s {
    RankEnvelopeSupplies { node_rank: r, host: h, cap_basis_points: c, free_bytes: f } =>
      join(["rank ", to_string(r), " on ", h as String, " supplies ", to_string(c),
            " bp (", to_string(byte_size_count(b: f)), " bytes free)"], "")
    RankEnvelopeBelowFloor { node_rank: r, host: h, cap_basis_points: c, floor_basis_points: fl } =>
      join(["rank ", to_string(r), " on ", h as String, " supplies only ", to_string(c),
            " bp, below the arm floor of ", to_string(fl), " bp"], "")
    RankEnvelopeUnread { node_rank: r, host: h, cause: cz } =>
      join(["rank ", to_string(r), " on ", h as String, " device memory UNREAD: ", cz], "")
    RankEnvelopeForeignImage { node_rank: r, host: h, observed_image: oi, expected_image: ei } =>
      join(["rank ", to_string(r), " on ", h as String, " observed under ", oi as String,
            " not ", ei as String], "")
    RankEnvelopeStaleAttempt { node_rank: r, host: h, observed_attempt: oa, expected_attempt: ea } =>
      join(["rank ", to_string(r), " on ", h as String, " observation belongs to attempt ", oa as String,
            " not ", ea as String], "")
  }
}

fn rank_standing_cap(s: RankEnvelopeStanding) -> Nat? {
  match s {
    RankEnvelopeSupplies { node_rank: _, host: _, cap_basis_points: c, free_bytes: _ } => Present { value: c }
    RankEnvelopeBelowFloor { node_rank: _, host: _, cap_basis_points: _, floor_basis_points: _ } => none
    RankEnvelopeUnread { node_rank: _, host: _, cause: _ } => none
    RankEnvelopeForeignImage { node_rank: _, host: _, observed_image: _, expected_image: _ } => none
    RankEnvelopeStaleAttempt { node_rank: _, host: _, observed_attempt: _, expected_attempt: _ } => none
  }
}

fn rank_standing_rank(s: RankEnvelopeStanding) -> Nat {
  match s {
    RankEnvelopeSupplies { node_rank: r, host: _, cap_basis_points: _, free_bytes: _ } => r
    RankEnvelopeBelowFloor { node_rank: r, host: _, cap_basis_points: _, floor_basis_points: _ } => r
    RankEnvelopeUnread { node_rank: r, host: _, cause: _ } => r
    RankEnvelopeForeignImage { node_rank: r, host: _, observed_image: _, expected_image: _ } => r
    RankEnvelopeStaleAttempt { node_rank: r, host: _, observed_attempt: _, expected_attempt: _ } => r
  }
}

// ── THE POPULATION STANDING IS A COMPLETENESS JOIN, NOT A COUNT ────────────────────────────────
//
// EVERY PLANNED RANK EXACTLY ONCE, MATCHED BY IDENTITY. A count equality would be satisfied by four
// readings of one host, which is the shape a fold over a mis-built node list actually produces -- and it
// is precisely the reading population that would have declared the 2026-09-10 launch admissible, because
// the rank that had memory was observed and the two that did not were not.
type StartupEnvelopePopulationStanding
  = EnvelopePopulationComplete { standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding> }
  | EnvelopePopulationIncomplete { standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>, missing_ranks: List<Nat>, cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn standing_for_rank(standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>, r: Nat) -> List<RankEnvelopeStanding> {
  filter(standings, s => rank_standing_rank(s: s) == r)
}

fn ranks_not_observed_exactly_once(nodes: List<ArmNode>, standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>) -> List<Nat> {
  map(
    filter(nodes, n => count(standing_for_rank(standings: standings, r: n.node_rank)) != 1),
    n => n.node_rank,
  )
}

fn envelope_population_standing(
  nodes: List<ArmNode>, standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>,
) -> StartupEnvelopePopulationStanding {
  let missing = ranks_not_observed_exactly_once(nodes: nodes, standings: standings)
  if count(nodes) == 0 {
    EnvelopePopulationIncomplete {
      standings: standings, missing_ranks: [],
      cause: "no planned ranks: an envelope over an empty population would admit anything" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if count(missing) != 0 {
    EnvelopePopulationIncomplete {
      standings: standings, missing_ranks: missing,
      cause: "some planned rank was not observed exactly once" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if count(standings) != count(nodes) {
    EnvelopePopulationIncomplete {
      standings: standings, missing_ranks: [],
      cause: "a standing was produced for a rank that is not in the plan" as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    EnvelopePopulationComplete { standings: standings }
  }
}

// ── THE RESOLUTION ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE GROUP'S ENVELOPE IS THE WEAKEST RANK'S, CAPPED BY THE OPERATOR'S CEILING. A tensor-parallel group is
// one allocation decision made four times with the same number, so a fraction any member cannot hold is a
// fraction the group cannot hold -- and the member that cannot hold it is the one that refuses, nine
// minutes before the rank that can notices.
//
// THE RESOLUTION NAMES ITS BINDING RANK. Without it the receipt says only which number was chosen, and the
// next question -- why this low -- has no answer but a re-run.
type ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope
  = ResolvedForAttempt {
      basis_points: Nat,
      binding_node_rank: Nat,
      policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy,
      attempt: NonEmptyStr,
      standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>,
    }
  | NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope { cause: NonEmptyStr, standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding> }

type EnvelopeMinAccum sole_constructor {
  cap: Nat
  node_rank: Nat
  any: Bool
}

fn envelope_min_step(acc: EnvelopeMinAccum, s: RankEnvelopeStanding) -> EnvelopeMinAccum {
  match rank_standing_cap(s: s) {
    Absent => acc
    Present { value: c } =>
      if !acc.any || c < acc.cap {
        EnvelopeMinAccum { cap: c, node_rank: rank_standing_rank(s: s), any: true }
      } else { acc }
  }
}

// A NON-SUPPLYING STANDING REFUSES THE WHOLE RESOLUTION RATHER THAN BEING SKIPPED. The minimum fold above
// ignores standings with no cap, which is correct arithmetic and would be catastrophic policy on its own:
// the minimum over the ranks that happened to answer is exactly the number that made 2026-09-10 look
// admissible. So the resolution requires every standing to supply before it consults the minimum.
fn unsupplied_standings(ss: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>) -> List<RankEnvelopeStanding> {
  filter(ss, s => match rank_standing_cap(s: s) {
    Absent => true
    Present { value: _ } => false
  })
}

fn resolve_device_memory_envelope(
  nodes: List<ArmNode>, standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>,
  policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy, attempt: NonEmptyStr,
) -> ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope {
  match envelope_population_standing(nodes: nodes, standings: standings) {
    EnvelopePopulationIncomplete { standings: ss, missing_ranks: _, cause: c } =>
      NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope { cause: c, standings: ss }
    EnvelopePopulationComplete { standings: ss } => {
      let unsupplied = unsupplied_standings(ss: ss)
      if count(unsupplied) != 0 {
        NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope {
          cause: join(["a planned rank does not supply the arm floor or was not readable: ",
                       join(map(unsupplied, s => rank_envelope_standing_text(s: s)), "; ")], "") as NonEmptyStr,
          standings: ss,
        }
      } else {
        let m = fold(ss, init: EnvelopeMinAccum { cap: 0, node_rank: 0, any: false }, f: (acc, s) => envelope_min_step(acc: acc, s: s))
        let resolved = nat_min(a: m.cap, b: policy.maximum_fraction_basis_points)
        if resolved < policy.minimum_fraction_basis_points {
          NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope {
            cause: join(["resolved ", to_string(resolved), " bp is below the arm floor of ",
                         to_string(policy.minimum_fraction_basis_points), " bp"], "") as NonEmptyStr,
            standings: ss,
          }
        } else {
          ResolvedForAttempt {
            basis_points: resolved,
            binding_node_rank: m.node_rank,
            policy: policy,
            attempt: attempt,
            standings: ss,
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// ── THE INITIAL NORMAL DEPLOYMENT DECIDES THE FRACTION AND VERIFIES IT, RATHER THAN DERIVING IT ─
//
// A DECIDED FRACTION AND A RESOLVED ONE ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS, AND THE FIRST NORMAL DEPLOYMENT WANTS
// THE FIRST. Resolution asks "what is the most this population can carry", which is the right question for
// an envelope that must not exceed supply and the wrong one for a launch an operator has already specified.
// 0.84 is an operator decision for the initial normal deployment (2026-09-11): conservative, known to clear
// the worst startup guard reading observed on this fleet, and explicitly not a claim of optimality.
//
// SO THE FRACTION LEAVES THE CRITICAL PATH AND THE OBSERVATION DOES NOT. Every planned rank is still read
// fresh, in the launch image, under the exact digest and attempt, with the same completeness join and the
// same refusals -- what changes is the question asked of those readings: not "what is the minimum cap" but
// "does every rank admit the decided fraction". A rank that cannot is a refusal, not a renegotiation, and
// the launch does not quietly drop to what that rank could have taken.
//
// THE FIRST LAUNCH IS WHAT RETIRES THIS ROW. A serving deployment produces a real startup memory profile,
// and a fraction derived from that profile is better than either a decided or a supply-resolved one. Until a
// rank has served, there is no such profile, which is why this is decided rather than derived and says so.
data glm_native_decided_basis_points: Nat = 8400

// THE DECIDED VALUE MUST BE EXPRESSIBLE AND WITHIN POLICY, and both are checked here rather than assumed by
// the caller: a decided fraction above the operator's own ceiling, below the arm's floor, or off the wire's
// granularity is a specification error and refuses before any rank is consulted.
fn decided_fraction_is_well_formed(decided: Nat, policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy) -> Bool {
  decided <= policy.maximum_fraction_basis_points
    && decided >= policy.minimum_fraction_basis_points
    && (decided / policy.granularity_basis_points) * policy.granularity_basis_points == decided
}

fn ranks_below_decided(ss: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>, decided: Nat) -> List<RankEnvelopeStanding> {
  filter(ss, s => match rank_standing_cap(s: s) {
    Absent => true
    Present { value: c } => c < decided
  })
}

fn admit_decided_envelope(
  nodes: List<ArmNode>, standings: List<RankEnvelopeStanding>,
  policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy, attempt: NonEmptyStr, decided: Nat,
) -> ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope {
  if !decided_fraction_is_well_formed(decided: decided, policy: policy) {
    NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope {
      cause: join(["the decided fraction ", to_string(decided),
                   " bp is outside this arm's policy or off the wire's granularity"], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      standings: standings,
    }
  } else {
    match envelope_population_standing(nodes: nodes, standings: standings) {
      EnvelopePopulationIncomplete { standings: ss, missing_ranks: _, cause: c } =>
        NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope { cause: c, standings: ss }
      EnvelopePopulationComplete { standings: ss } => {
        let short = ranks_below_decided(ss: ss, decided: decided)
        if count(short) != 0 {
          NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope {
            cause: join(["a planned rank cannot admit the decided fraction of ", to_string(decided), " bp: ",
                         join(map(short, s => rank_envelope_standing_text(s: s)), "; ")], "") as NonEmptyStr,
            standings: ss,
          }
        } else {
          ResolvedForAttempt {
            basis_points: decided,
            binding_node_rank: 0,
            policy: policy,
            attempt: attempt,
            standings: ss,
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

func resolve_envelope_from_fleet(
  ctx: SparkRemoteExecutionContext, nodes: List<ArmNode>, image: NonEmptyStr,
  attempt: NonEmptyStr, policy: DeviceMemoryEnvelopePolicy,
) -> ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope
  uses net: Network
{
  let readings = observe_device_memory_population(ctx: ctx, nodes: nodes, image: image, attempt: attempt)
  admit_decided_envelope(
    nodes: nodes,
    standings: map(readings, r => rank_envelope_standing(
      reading: r, policy: policy, expected_image: image, expected_attempt: attempt)),
    policy: policy,
    attempt: attempt,
    decided: glm_native_decided_basis_points,
  )
}

// ── THE NATIVE ARM'S PLAN IS PRODUCED FROM A RESOLVED ENVELOPE AND FROM NOTHING ELSE ───────────
//
// THIS IS WHERE THE WALL IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN PROCEDURAL. gunbc.spark.serving_arm_launch renders argv
// from a fraction it receives, and this is the only named producer of the native four-rank plan -- so the
// fraction a launch carries cannot exist without a ResolvedForAttempt, and a ResolvedForAttempt cannot
// exist without one fresh supplying reading per planned rank. The previous shape had a data row in the
// renderer, which any caller could reach and every caller did.
//
// THE NODE ROSTER IS READ TWICE AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANT WORK. The observation population must be the
// PLANNED ranks, so the roster is needed before the plan exists; the plan then derives its own roster from
// the same function over the same group, so the two agree by construction rather than by a check. What
// would be redundant is carrying the first roster into the second, which would make the plan's ranks a
// parameter and let a caller plan for hosts it did not observe.
fn glm_native_tp4_nodes() -> ArmNodeRoster {
  arm_node_roster(group: FabricGroupB, expected_ranks: serving_topology_rank_count(t: FourSparkTp4))
}

// ── THE PLAN IS BUILT FROM A SELECTED VALUE AND AN AUTHORISATION THAT COVERS IT ────────────────
//
// A PREVIOUS REVISION CONSTRUCTED THE TUPLE HERE, naming the checkpoint, runtime, topology and intent inline.
// That made this function a fourth author of the deployment: the decision parameters described one engine,
// the launch renderer another, and this constructed a third, all agreeing by coincidence. A review named it
// the central authority break -- changing the selected runtime had no necessary effect on the image launched.
//
// SO THE SUBJECT ARRIVES ALREADY SELECTED AND ALREADY AUTHORISED, and this function contributes exactly one
// thing the selection cannot: the memory fraction, which is resolved per attempt from observed supply and
// therefore cannot live in a profile whose identity must stay stable.
//
// THE AUTHORISATION IS CHECKED AGAINST THE SELECTION'S STRUCTURAL IDENTITY, so one minted for a different
// checkpoint, runtime, topology, accelerator, profile or intent does not cover this launch. The durable claim
// the transaction takes is a MUTEX and answers a different question entirely -- whether another attempt is
// mid-flight -- which is why it cannot stand in for this.
fn glm_native_tp4_launch_plan_for(
  selected: SelectedServingDeployment,
  authorization: SelectedDeploymentAuthorization,
  envelope: ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope,
) -> ArmLaunchPlan {
  if !authorization_covers(a: authorization, s: selected) {
    ArmLaunchRefused {
      cause: join([
        "the authorisation does not cover this selection: granted for ",
        authorization.selected_identity as String, ", asked for ", selected_identity_of(s: selected) as String,
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else {
    match envelope {
      NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope { cause: c, standings: _ } => ArmLaunchRefused {
        cause: join(["no feasible device memory envelope, so there is no plan: ", c as String], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      }
      ResolvedForAttempt { basis_points: bp, binding_node_rank: _, policy: _, attempt: _, standings: _ } =>
        plan_arm_launch_for(
          checkpoint: selected.subject.checkpoint,
          runtime: selected.subject.runtime,
          topology: selected.subject.topology,
          group: FabricGroupB,
          intent: if selected.intent_is_normal_serving { ArmNormalServing } else { ArmBoundedExperiment },
          profile: selected.subject.profile,
          utilization_basis_points: bp,
        )
    }
  }
}

// THE NATIVE ARM'S OWN SELECTION, produced through the admission fold rather than constructed, so a subject
// the compatibility roster refuses cannot reach a plan even here. It asks for a bounded experiment because no
// execution has shown this subject serving -- which is exactly what select_serving_deployment enforces, and
// today this subject is ExecutionIncompatible, so the selection refuses and there is no plan at all.
fn glm_native_tp4_selection() -> SelectionAdmission {
  select_serving_deployment(
    subject: glm_native_on_official_sm121_tp4,
    standing: glm_native_official_sm121_tp4_standing,
    intent_is_normal_serving: false,
  )
}

fn glm_native_tp4_launch_plan(envelope: ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope) -> ArmLaunchPlan {
  match glm_native_tp4_selection() {
    SelectionRefused { cause: c } => ArmLaunchRefused { cause: c }
    SelectionAdmitted { selected: sel } =>
      glm_native_tp4_launch_plan_for(
        selected: sel,
        authorization: authorize_selection(s: sel, granted_for_normal_serving: false),
        envelope: envelope,
      )
  }
}

// THE IMAGE THE PROBE RUNS IS THE IMAGE THE LAUNCH RUNS, READ FROM THE ARM'S OWN ROW. Passing it as a
// caller argument would let an observation be taken under one digest and a launch performed under another,
// which is the mismatch RankEnvelopeForeignImage exists to catch -- and a check is a poor substitute for
// there being only one place the value comes from.
fn glm_native_tp4_runtime_image() -> NonEmptyStr {
  runtime_artifact_wire(a: vllm_openai_glm53_flash_row.artifact)
}

func glm_native_tp4_resolve_envelope(
  ctx: SparkRemoteExecutionContext, nodes: List<ArmNode>, attempt: NonEmptyStr,
) -> ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope
  uses net: Network
{
  resolve_envelope_from_fleet(
    ctx: ctx, nodes: nodes, image: glm_native_tp4_runtime_image(),
    attempt: attempt, policy: glm_native_device_memory_policy,
  )
}

fn resolved_envelope_text(e: ResolvedDeviceMemoryEnvelope) -> String {
  match e {
    NoFeasibleDeviceMemoryEnvelope { cause: c, standings: ss } =>
      join(["NO FEASIBLE ENVELOPE: ", c as String, "\n",
            join(map(ss, s => rank_envelope_standing_text(s: s)), "\n")], "")
    ResolvedForAttempt { basis_points: bp, binding_node_rank: r, policy: _, attempt: a, standings: ss } =>
      join(["resolved ", to_string(bp), " bp for attempt ", a as String,
            " bound by rank ", to_string(r), "\n",
            join(map(ss, s => rank_envelope_standing_text(s: s)), "\n")], "")
  }
}
